<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albertino Mussato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1261">1261</a>–<a href="/years/1329">1329</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albertino Mussato was a Paduan statesman, historian and poet, an early figure of Italian humanism. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tragedy Ecerinis, on the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>, written in the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a>, warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> against Cangrande della Scala and earned him the poet's laurel in 1315. He died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mussato was born in the Gazzo district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1261, around the time of the grape harvest, as he recorded in an autobiographical poem of 1317. His father, Giovanni Cavalieri, was a court messenger. Rumour made him the son of Viviano Muso, the family's protector and his godfather, whose name he took, probably as a tribute. After Muso died, by 1276–7, the family fell into poverty and Mussato supported it by copying books for students. His teacher was the Paduan poet Lovato Lovati, who had lived through the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mussato trained as a lawyer and held many offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, sitting in its great council. In 1297 he became podestà of Lendinara. Around 1300 he went as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> and used the mission to win the abbacy of Santa Giustina for his brother Gualpertino, and in April 1301 he served six months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> as Executor of Judicial Ordinances. He later acted as an ambassador between Padua and Emperor Henry VII.</p><p>Fearing tyranny, he favoured the commune and saw Cangrande della Scala as a new Ezzelino. A rising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>'s rulers in April 1314 forced him briefly to flee to Vigodarzere. In 1318, after Niccolò da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> attacked Gualpertino, he went into exile, and in 1325 the Carrara family banished him for good. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a> on 31 May 1329, aged 68.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ecerinis, written around 1314, was modelled on the Senecan tragedies Thyestes and Octavia, plays that Lovati had brought to light, and was the first play composed in a classical metre since antiquity. Instead of myth it dramatised northern Italian history, drawing on Rolandino of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>'s chronicle. Mussato received the poet's laurel on 5 December 1315 for it and for his Historia Augusta, an account of Henry VII's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1310 to 1313.</p><p>He also wrote a history of Italian affairs after Henry VII and many letters, some in verse. In a 1317 exchange of letters with the Dominican friar Giovannino of Mantua he defended poetry as inspired by God, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. The humanist Salutati later called Mussato of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> the first cultivator of eloquence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> echoed his defence of ancient poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albertino Mussato” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The judge Lovato Lovati studied the metres of Seneca’s tragedies, and <b>Albertino Mussato</b> wrote a Latin tragedy in Seneca’s manner, the Ecerinis, on the tyranny of Ezzelino III da Romano, for which Padua crowned him with laurel in 1315.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the commune flourished: the basilica of Saint Anthony was built, Giotto painted the Scrovegni Chapel around 1305, and the scholar <b>Albertino Mussato</b> revived classical Latin drama.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.22,12.28&amp;year=1305" aria-label="Chioggia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4842 568 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Albertino Mussato"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5292 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5292 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.22,12.28&amp;year=1305">What was here in 1305? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
